## TL;DR  
Add a **root-level `sky` object** (or call `map.setSky()`) and give it a colour, a horizon colour and fog-related blends.  
The fog is part of the sky, not a separate `fog` layer, and it only becomes visible when **3-D terrain** is on and the camera is pitched.

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map (any style that already has a raster-dem source)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // look at the terrain from the side
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true          // needed for smooth sky/terrain rendering
});

// 2️⃣  Wait until the style (and the DEM source) is ready
map.on('load', () => {
  // ---- 3-D terrain -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // your DEM source
    tileSize: 256,
    maxzoom: 12
  });

  // Exaggeration is optional – 1 = true scale
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

  // ---- SKY + HAZE --------------------------------------------------
  // You can either put the object directly into the style JSON
  // or call the API as shown here.
  map.setSky({
    // overall sky colour (looks like a clear-day blue)
    'sky-color': '#80c0ff',

    // colour of the horizon line – a little lighter than the sky
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

    // colour of the fog that appears where the ground meets the sky
    // (usually a desaturated blue-gray)
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',

    // how quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon colour
    // 0 = hard line, 1 = very smooth
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // how much the fog colour blends into the horizon colour
    // 0 = no fog, 1 = fog covers the whole horizon
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

    // how far the fog reaches down onto the terrain
    // 0 = fog only at the very top, 1 = fog reaches the ground
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
  });

  // (optional) you can also add a background layer so the page colour
  // never shows through when the camera looks straight up.
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'bg',
    type: 'background',
    paint: { 'background-color': '#80c0ff' }   // same as sky-color
  });
});
```

---

## Why the page background appears

* **`sky` is not a layer.** In MapLibre GL JS the sky lives at the *root* of the style, not in the `layers` array. If you try to add a `type: "sky"` layer or use Mapbox-only properties (`sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-sun`, …) nothing will happen and you’ll just see the page’s background colour above the horizon.

* **Fog lives inside the sky object.** There is no `map.setFog()` or a `fog` layer type. The three fog-related properties (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) are part of the sky definition.

* **Terrain must be on.** Fog is only rendered when a `raster-dem` source is active via `map.setTerrain()`. If terrain is disabled the sky will render, but the fog will never appear.

---

## Full list of sky properties (MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6)

| Property                | Description | Typical values |
|-------------------------|-------------|----------------|
| `sky-color`             | Base colour of the sky (far from the horizon). | `#80c0ff`, `#199EF3` |
| `horizon-color`         | Colour of the line where sky meets ground. | `#ffffff` |
| `fog-color`             | Colour of the atmospheric haze that blends with the horizon. | `#c0d8ff` |
| `sky-horizon-blend`     | 0-1 blend factor between `sky-color` and `horizon-color`. | `0.3-0.7` |
| `horizon-fog-blend`     | 0-1 blend factor between `horizon-color` and `fog-color`. | `0.2-0.6` |
| `fog-ground-blend`      | 0-1 blend factor that pushes the fog down onto the terrain. | `0.3-0.8` |

> **Note:** `sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-sun`, `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity`, and any other “Mapbox-only” sky properties are ignored by MapLibre.

---

## Adding the sky via style JSON (alternative)

If you prefer to keep everything in the style file, just add a top-level `sky` object:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ],
  "terrain": { "source": "terrain-dem", "exaggeration": 1 },

  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#80c0ff",
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.4,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  }
}
```

When the style loads, MapLibre will automatically render the sky and fog.

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Only a solid colour above the horizon** | No `sky` object (or you added a `type: "sky"` layer). | Use `map.setSky()` or a top-level `sky` object. |
| **Fog never appears** | Terrain not set, or `fog-color` is fully transparent. | Call `map.setTerrain()` first and give `fog-color` an opaque value (e.g., `rgba(200,216,255,0.8)`). |
| **Sky looks too “flat” (hard line at horizon)** | `sky-horizon-blend` left at default `0`. | Raise `sky-horizon-blend` to ~0.5. |
| **Fog looks too thick / covers the whole map** | `horizon-fog-blend` or `fog-ground-blend` set to 1. | Reduce those values (0.2-0.5 is usually enough). |
| **Sky colour changes when you rotate the map** | `hillshade-illumination-anchor` set to `"map"` (only affects hillshade). | No impact on sky; just keep sky-related properties independent of bearing. |

---

## Quick checklist to get a proper sky & haze

1. **MapLibre version** – ≥ 5.6 (sky support was added in 5.6).  
2. **Terrain source** – a `raster-dem` source added and `map.setTerrain()` called.  
3. **Pitch** – any pitch > 0 (you already have 70°).  
4. **Sky definition** – either `map.setSky({ … })` *or* a top-level `"sky"` object in the style.  
5. **Fog colours & blends** – set `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`.  
6. **Background layer (optional)** – add a `background` layer with the same colour as `sky-color` to guarantee a colour when the camera looks straight up.

That’s it! After these steps the area above the horizon will be a smooth blue sky with a gentle haze that meets the terrain, giving your 3-D map a realistic atmosphere. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze that blends into the terrain.

---

## 1. Why the background is empty

* `map.setTerrain()` creates the 3-D mesh, but **sky is not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style.
* If you never add a `sky` object (or call `map.setSky()`), the renderer just draws the HTML page background behind the terrain.
* Fog (the haze that makes the ground fade into the sky) is also part of the `sky` object, not a separate `fog` layer.

So the fix is: **add a `sky` object** (or call `map.setSky()`) and configure its colors and blend factors.

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // or your own style URL
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // lon, lat
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // needed for smooth terrain shading
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the DEM source and enable terrain
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // DEM source – replace with your own URL if needed
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // <-- your DEM TileJSON
      tileSize: 256,
      // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (default is mapbox)
    });

    // Turn the DEM into 3-D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add a sky (this is the missing piece)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // The sky is a vertical gradient that blends into the horizon.
      // You can tweak any of these values to get the look you want.
      'sky-type': 'gradient',               // only “gradient” is supported in MapLibre
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',               // light-blue zenith (mid-day sky)
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,             // how quickly the zenith fades to horizon
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',           // near-white horizon line
      // -------------------------------------------------
      // 4️⃣  Add fog (the haze that meets the ground)
      // -------------------------------------------------
      'fog-color': '#cce0ff',               // same hue as sky, a bit lighter
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,             // how far the fog reaches up from the horizon
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5               // how quickly fog fades into the terrain
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Remove or make transparent the background layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // If your style has a background layer with a solid color,
    // set its paint to transparent so the sky shows through.
    const bg = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'background');
    if (bg) {
      map.setPaintProperty(bg.id, 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣  Add any other layers you need (hillshade, imagery, etc.)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example hillshade layer (softened):
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What it adds | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **`map.setTerrain()`** | 3-D mesh from your `raster-dem` source | Gives you the relief you already see |
| **`map.setSky({ … })`** | A gradient sky + fog (haze) | Replaces the plain page background |
| **`sky-type: "gradient"`** | The only sky type MapLibre supports (no “atmosphere” or “sun” properties) |
| **`sky-color` / `horizon-color`** | Controls the zenith and horizon hues | Pick any palette – e.g., sunrise, night, etc. |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | How quickly the zenith fades into the horizon | 0 = hard line, 1 = very smooth |
| **`fog-color`** | Colour of the haze that meets the ground | Usually a lighter version of the sky colour |
| **`horizon-fog-blend`** | How far the fog extends upward from the horizon | 0 = no fog, 1 = fog reaches the zenith |
| **`fog-ground-blend`** | How quickly fog fades into the terrain | 0 = fog stops at the ground, 1 = fog fully covers low terrain |
| **Transparent background layer** | Guarantees the sky is visible behind everything | Some styles set a solid `background-color`; making it transparent avoids a “white box” behind the sky |

---

## 3. Fine-tuning the look

| Parameter | Typical range | Visual effect |
|-----------|---------------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | `#87CEEB` (day) → `#001d3d` (dusk) | Overall sky hue |
| `horizon-color` | Near-white for bright day, `#ffddaa` for sunrise, `#222222` for night | Colour of the line where sky meets terrain |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | **0.2 – 0.7** | Small values give a crisp horizon; larger values make a soft gradient |
| `fog-color` | Same hue as `sky-color` but lighter (`rgba(255,255,255,0.2)` for mist) | Colour of the haze |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | **0.1 – 0.5** | Low values → thin mist just above the horizon; higher values → thick fog that climbs up |
| `fog-ground-blend` | **0.3 – 0.8** | Low values → fog stops at the base of hills; high values → low valleys get completely shrouded |

**Tip:** Start with the defaults shown in the example, then adjust one property at a time while watching the map. Small changes (≈0.05) can make a big visual difference.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** | `map.setSky()` called **before** the map is loaded, or the style already contains a `sky` object that is later overwritten. | Call `map.setSky()` inside `map.on('load', …)` **after** terrain is set, or edit the style JSON to include a `sky` object at the root. |
| **Fog stays black / invisible** | `fog-color` is fully transparent or the same as `sky-color` with `horizon-fog-blend` = 0. | Give `fog-color` an alpha (`rgba(200,220,255,0.6)`) and set `horizon-fog-blend` > 0. |
| **Background still shows** | A `background` layer with an opaque color sits on top of the sky. | Set its `background-color` to `rgba(0,0,0,0)` (transparent) or remove the layer. |
| **Terrain looks flat even with pitch** | Camera pitch is still `0` or the map is rendered in a container that forces a 2-D view (e.g., CSS `transform: rotateX(0deg)`). | Ensure `pitch` is set (e.g., `map.easeTo({pitch:70})`) and the container has no CSS that resets 3-D transforms. |
| **Performance drops dramatically** | Too many high-resolution DEM tiles + heavy hillshade + sky + many draped raster layers. | Reduce DEM `maxzoom` (or use lower-resolution tiles at far zoom), keep only one hillshade layer, and consider `hillshade-exaggeration` < 0.5. |

---

## 5. Full-style JSON alternative (if you prefer editing the style file)

If you manage your map via a static style JSON (e.g., hosted on a CDN), you can embed the sky directly:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "terrain-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",
      "tileSize": 256
    },
    "...": { "...": "..." }
  },
  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain-dem",
    "exaggeration": 1
  },
  "sky": {
    "sky-type": "gradient",
    "sky-color": "#87CEEB",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "fog-color": "#cce0ff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.3,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "background",
      "type": "background",
      "paint": { "background-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0)" }
    },
    {
      "id": "hillshade",
      "type": "hillshade",
      "source": "terrain-dem",
      "paint": {
        "hillshade-method": "multidirectional",
        "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.4,
        "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "map",
        "hillshade-shadow-color": "rgba(30,40,80,0.45)",
        "hillshade-highlight-color": "rgba(255,240,200,0.4)"
      }
    },
    "... other layers ..."
  ]
}
```

Load this style with `new maplibregl.Map({style: 'my-style.json', …})`. The sky will appear automatically; you no longer need `map.setSky()` in JavaScript.

---

## 6. Quick checklist to verify everything works

1. **Terrain is on** – `map.getTerrain()` returns an object with `source: "terrain-dem"`.
2. **Pitch > 0** – `map.getPitch()` shows a value (e.g., 70).
3. **Sky object exists** – `map.getStyle().sky` is defined and contains the properties you set.
4. **Background layer is transparent** – `map.getPaintProperty('background', 'background-color')` returns `rgba(0,0,0,0)`.
5. **Fog is visible** – you should see a subtle gradient that starts at the horizon and fades into the terrain. Adjust `horizon-fog-blend` / `fog-ground-blend` if it looks too thin or too thick.

