Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native to **MapLibre GL JS** – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1️⃣  Why the sky is missing

* The map already has **terrain** (`map.setTerrain(...)`) and a **pitch** (`70°`).  
* The sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the *root* of the style, just like `terrain`.  
* If you never add a `sky` object (or call `map.setSky()`), the renderer falls back to the page’s CSS background, which looks like a flat white/gray rectangle.

So the fix is simply: **add a sky object** (or call `map.setSky()`) and give it the fog parameters you want.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- DEM source (raster-dem) ---------------------------------
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          // Example DEM – replace with your own
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12
        },
        // ---- Imagery source (optional) -------------------------------
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 19
        }
      },
      // -------------------------------------------------
      // 2️⃣  Add the background (optional) – keep it *below* sky
      // -------------------------------------------------
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'background',
          type: 'background',
          paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' }   // just a neutral ground colour
        },
        {
          id: 'satellite',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'sat'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [ -122.447303, 37.753574 ], // San Francisco (lon,lat)
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // <-- you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // required for terrain + sky
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Wait for the style to load before adding terrain / sky
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 3a️⃣  Enable terrain ---------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      exaggeration: 1.0          // tweak if you want more “relief”
    });

    // ---- 3b️⃣  Add the sky (the missing piece) ----------------
    map.setSky({
      // ---- Sky colour (the “atmosphere”) --------------------
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',          // light blue – you can use any CSS colour
      // ---- Horizon colour (where sky meets ground) ---------
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // pure white works well
      // ---- How the sky blends into the horizon -------------
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // 0 = hard line, 1 = fully blended
      // ---- Fog (haze) – this is what gives the “ground-meets-sky” effect
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',         // a slightly desaturated blue
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,       // how quickly fog fades near the horizon
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5         // how far the fog reaches down onto the terrain
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  (Optional) Add a hillshade or color-relief layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example hillshade – you can skip this if you already have one
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What it adds | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **`map.setTerrain()`** | 3-D mesh from the `raster-dem` source | Gives you the relief you already see |
| **`map.setSky({ … })`** | Root-level sky object | Replaces the plain page background with a sky gradient |
| **`sky-color`** | Base colour of the sky (high up) | Light blue, but any colour works |
| **`horizon-color`** | Colour exactly at the horizon line | Usually a lighter version of `sky-color` or white |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | Controls how smoothly the two colours merge | 0 = hard line, 1 = fully blended |
| **`fog-color`** | Colour of the atmospheric haze (the “fog”) | A slightly desaturated blue gives a natural look |
| **`horizon-fog-blend`** | How quickly the fog fades near the horizon | Small values keep a crisp horizon, larger values give a soft glow |
| **`fog-ground-blend`** | How far the fog reaches down onto the terrain | 0 = no fog on ground, 1 = fog covers the whole terrain |

> **Tip:** The sky only becomes visible when the map is **pitched** (your `70°` is perfect) **and** terrain is active. If you ever set `pitch: 0` you’ll see the sky disappear again – that’s expected.

---

## 3️⃣  Adding the sky directly in the **style JSON** (alternative)

If you prefer to keep everything in the style object (e.g. when you load a style from a URL), you can embed the sky under the top-level `"sky"` key instead of calling `map.setSky()`:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ],
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#87CEEB",
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.3,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  }
}
```

When the style loads, MapLibre will automatically render the sky. The JavaScript `map.setSky()` call does the same thing at runtime, which is handy if you want to toggle or recolour the sky on the fly.

---

## 4️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky still missing** | `map.setSky()` called **before** the `load` event, or before terrain is set. | Call `setSky` **inside** `map.on('load', …)` **after** `setTerrain`. |
| **Background colour still shows** | A `background` layer placed **above** the sky (layers are drawn on top of the sky). | Keep the `background` layer **first** in the `layers` array, or remove it. |
| **Fog looks too strong / too weak** | Wrong blend values. | Adjust `horizon-fog-blend` (near-horizon softness) and `fog-ground-blend` (how far down the fog reaches). |
| **Sky colour looks washed out** | Using a very light `sky-color` together with a white `horizon-color`. | Choose a slightly deeper sky colour (e.g. `#87CEEB` or `#6fa8dc`) and keep the horizon lighter. |
| **No change after editing the style** | Browser cache – the style JSON is cached. | Append a query string (`?v=2`) to the style URL or clear the cache. |
| **MapLibre version older than 5.5** | `sky` and `color-relief` were introduced in 5.5/5.6. | Upgrade to at least **5.6** (the latest stable release). |

---

## 5️⃣  Quick “tweak-the-sky” UI (optional)

If you want to let users play with the sky colours or fog intensity, you can expose the same properties via `map.setSky()` at any time:

```js
// Example: a simple slider that changes fog intensity
document.getElementById('fogBlend').addEventListener('input', e => {
  const val = Number(e.target.value); // 0 … 1
  const current = map.getSky();        // returns the current sky object
  map.setSky({
    ...current,
    'fog-ground-blend': val
  });
});
```

All sky properties are **reactive** – the map redraws instantly.

---

## 6️⃣  Recap – What you need to do

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem', exaggeration:1})`).  
2. **Pitch the map** (you already have `70°`).  
3. **Add a sky object** – either via `map.setSky({ … })` *after* the map loads, **or** embed a `"sky"` block in your style JSON.  
4. **Configure fog** (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) to get the desired haze.  
5. Ensure no layer sits **above** the sky (background layers should be first).  

That’s it – the empty page background will be replaced by a realistic sky that fades into a gentle fog, giving your 3-D terrain a proper horizon. 🎈

Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the blank “page background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native to **MapLibre GL JS** – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1️⃣ Why the blank horizon appears

* The map already has **3-D terrain** (`map.setTerrain(...)`) and a **pitched camera** – that’s why the ground looks three-dimensional.  
* **Sky** in MapLibre is **not a layer**; it is a **root-level style object** (or set via `map.setSky()`).  
* If you never add a sky object, the renderer simply draws the HTML page background (usually white or whatever your page’s CSS sets) above the terrain’s horizon.

So the fix is: **add a sky object** (or call `map.setSky()`) and configure its fog/haze parameters.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – terrain + sky + haze</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // base style (you can use your own)
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco, for example
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true     // needed for smooth terrain rendering
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (raster-dem) and enable terrain
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // DEM source – replace the URL with your own tiles if you have them
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/tiles.json',
      tileSize: 256,
      // optional: if your DEM uses a non-default encoding, set it here
      // encoding: 'terrarium'
    });

    // Turn the DEM into 3-D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.0 });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add a sky object (the “real sky”)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // Overall sky colour – a light blue works well for a clear day
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',          // #87CEEB = sky-blue

      // Colour of the horizon line (where sky meets ground)
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // pure white gives a crisp horizon

      // How quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon colour
      // 0 = abrupt line, 1 = very gradual gradient
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

      // -------------------------------------------------
      // 4️⃣  Fog / haze – this is what gives the “ground-meets-sky” glow
      // -------------------------------------------------
      // Colour of the fog (usually a desaturated version of the sky colour)
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a soft, slightly paler blue

      // How much the fog colour blends into the horizon colour
      // 0 = no fog at the horizon, 1 = horizon fully fogged
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

      // How much the fog colour blends into the ground (terrain)
      // 0 = fog only in the sky, 1 = fog reaches the ground
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Add a hillshade or colour-relief layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example hillshade – you can keep or remove it
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1** | Loads a base style (any style works). |
| **2** | Adds a `raster-dem` source and calls `map.setTerrain()`. This creates the 3-D mesh. |
| **3** | Calls `map.setSky()` – this injects a **root-level `sky` object** into the style. |
| **4** | The three fog properties (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) create the haze that smoothly blends the terrain into the sky. |
| **5** | (Optional) Adds a hillshade layer so you can see the terrain relief under the sky. |

> **Important:** The sky will **only appear** when **terrain is active** *and* the camera **pitch is > 0**. If you set `pitch: 0` you’ll see a flat map again, even with a sky object.

---

## 3️⃣ Understanding the sky & fog properties

| Property | Description | Typical values |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| `sky-color` | Base colour of the sky dome (high up). | Light blue `#87CEEB`, sunset orange `#ffb347`, night `#0b1d2e` |
| `horizon-color` | Colour exactly at the horizon line. | Usually white or a very pale version of `sky-color`. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Gradient strength between `sky-color` and `horizon-color`. | `0.3-0.7` works well; higher = smoother transition. |
| `fog-color` | Colour of the atmospheric fog that sits between sky and ground. | Slightly desaturated version of `sky-color`. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | How much fog is mixed into the horizon colour. | `0-0.5` – larger values give a “misty” horizon. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How far the fog reaches down onto the terrain. | `0-0.5` – larger values make the ground look hazier. |

Play with these numbers until you get the look you want. A quick tip: start with the sky-blue palette above, then **increase** `horizon-fog-blend` and `fog-ground-blend` together to make the fog thicker, or **decrease** them for a crisp, clear day.

---

## 4️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** (still page background) | • `map.setTerrain()` not called *or* terrain source failed to load.<br>• Camera pitch is `0`.<br>• Using a MapLibre version older than **v2.0** (sky was added in v2). | Verify the DEM source loads (check the network tab). Set `pitch` > 0. Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (`npm i maplibre-gl@latest` or CDN). |
| **Fog looks like a solid band at the horizon** | `horizon-fog-blend` set to `1` (or too high) and `sky-horizon-blend` low. | Reduce `horizon-fog-blend` to ≤ 0.4 and increase `sky-horizon-blend`. |
| **Terrain looks flat even with pitch** | Terrain source is a **raster** (image) instead of **raster-dem**, or `map.setTerrain(null)` was called later. | Ensure the source type is `"raster-dem"` and that you never call `map.setTerrain(null)` after enabling it. |
| **Sky colour is wrong (e.g., black)** | Using a `sky-type` property (Mapbox-only) or a typo in the property name. | Remove any `sky-type` or `sky-atmosphere-*` properties – they are ignored. Use only the six properties listed above. |
| **Performance drops dramatically** | Stacking many hillshade layers *and* a dense colour-relief layer on top of terrain. | Keep only one hillshade (use `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` to soften). Use colour-relief only if you need hypsometric tint; otherwise, keep the layer count low. |

---

## 5️⃣ Quick “add-sky-to-an-existing-map” snippet

If you already have a map with terrain and just need to drop in a sky, you can do it in **one line** after the map has loaded:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // make sure terrain is already on
  map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

  // add sky (you can tweak the colours later)
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
  });
});
```

That’s it – the blank background disappears and you get a realistic sky with a gentle haze.

---

